## 1. Introduction
The development of renewable and clean energy can reduce the dependence on fossil energy and solve the problem of environmental pollution. Hydrogen has become one of the most potential energy substitutes due to its wide range of sources, environmental friendliness, and high energy density [1-3]. Electrochemical water splitting can convert unsustainable electrical energy (wind energy, solar energy, ocean energy, etc.) into storable chemical energy (hydrogen energy). However, the water splitting efficiency is slow due to the anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) involving multiple electron transfers and the formation of $\scriptstyle0=0$ bond [4-6]. Therefore, the development of efficient electrocatalysts to accelerate reaction rates or finding other anodic reactions with lower potential to replace the OER can facilitate the practical production of hydrogen.
Among the numerous anode reactions, urea oxidation reaction (UOR) not only degrades wastewater but also requires a lower thermodynamic voltage (0.37 V) to generate hydrogen, becoming the most promising alternative reaction for OER [7,8]. Nevertheless, the UOR is also kinetically constrained due to six-electron transfer and complex intermediate evolution processes [9,10]. Thus, designing efficient OER and UOR bifunctional electrocatalysts can greatly facilitate large-scale hydrogen production.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of blooming star materials, are constructed from metal ions or clusters and organic ligands. MOFs catalysts have recently attracted tremendous research interest in the fields of heterogeneous catalysis, due porous structure, tunable composition, and large specific surface area [11,12]. Particularly, MOFs have become one of the best electrocatalysts for OER and UOR, as the unique properties. Although considerable progress has been accomplished, achieving efficient electrocatalytic activity for MOFs is currently a challenge due to its poor electrical conductivity and low exposure active sites. Numerous strategies have been developed to enhance electrocatalytic activity, such as heteroatom doping, morphology regulation, and interface engineering [13-15]. Further, bimetallic MOFs (heteroatom doping) have received extensive research interest in electrocatalysis due to the tunability and flexibility of coordinating metal ions [16]. In addition to heteroatom modification, it is well known that ultrathin MOFs nanosheets with large specific surface area (morphology regulation) can ensure fast mass and electron transfer, and expose numerous metal ions on the surface as active sites, which can improve the utilization of active sites and is expected to enhance catalytic activity.
On the other hand, to further enhance the catalytic performance, developing heterogeneous interfaces (interface engineering) between different components is also another novel approach to improve the conductivity or adjust the electronic structure for optimizing electrocatalytic activity [17-20]. For example, metal sulfide/metal oxide [21,22], metal sulfide/MOFs [23,24], and metal sulfide/LDH [25,26] have shown excellent catalytic performance, providing an appealing platform for the microstructural design of heterogeneous catalysts. More obviously, metal sulfides have been extensively studied in alkaline systems due to their high intrinsic properties, low-cost and conveniently synthesis. Introducing heterogeneous species (metal sulfides) on MOFs can improve electrical conductivity, tune the microstructure, and increasing the surface area [24,27]. More importantly, the synergistic effect between different components may improve the interfacial electrons or form defects to optimize the adsorption energy of the intermediates, which ultimately enhances the intrinsic catalytic activity [28,29]. Based on the above analysis, it is particularly important to rationally design heterostructures through comprehensive consideration of morphology regulation, adsorption energy optimization and active site construction.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Iron chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , CAS number: 10025-77- $1,\geq99.00\%$ , Cobalt chloride hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , CAS number: 7791-13-1, $98.00\%$ , N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, CAS number: 68-12-2, $299.00\%$ ), hydrochloric acid (HCl, CAS number: 7647-01-0, $36.46\%$ , ethanol (CAS number: 64-17-5, $299.70\%$ , thioacetamide (TAA, CAS number: 62-55-5, $299.00\%$ and urea (CAS number: 57-13-6, $99.00\%$ were obtained from Sinopharm (Shanxi, China). Potassium hydroxide (KOH, CAS number: 1310- 58-3, $85.00\%$ ), Sodium Chloride (NaCl, CAS number: 7647-14-5, $299.50\%$ , potassium bicarbonate $(\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ , CAS number: 298-14- $5,\geq99.50\%$ ), glucose ( $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{6}H_{12}O_{6}}$ , CAS number: 50-99-7, $299.50\%$ ), and terephthalic acid (BDC, CAS number: 100-21-0, $99.00\%$ were purchased from Aladdin Ltd (Shanghai, China). NF was from The Source of Power Battery Material Co., Ltd. (Shanxi, China). All chemical reagents used were not further purified.
## 2.2. Preparation of the catalysts
Preparation of Co–MOF/NF and CoFe–MOF/NF: before the experiment, NF was washed with 2 M HCl, ultrapure water, and ethanol under ultrasound. Co–MOF/NF was synthesized by combining previous literature [30,31]. BDC $\left(0.50\mathrm{\mmol}\right).$ , ${\mathsf{C o C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ $(0.50~\mathrm{\mmol})$ , DMF $\mathrm{10~\mL)}$ , ethanol $(2\ \mathrm{\mL})$ , ultrapure water $(2~\mathrm{mL})$ ) and the washed NF $1\mathrm{cm}\times3\mathrm{cm}$ ) were transferred into a $23~\mathrm{mL}$ autoclave. Then, the autoclave was sealed and maintained at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ in an oven. After the reaction, NF was rinsed with ethanol and ultrapure water, and then dried in $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to produce the Co–MOF/NF. The preparation of CoFe–MOF/NF was similar to Co–MOF/NF, only $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\left(0.10\mathrm{mmol}\right)^{\cdot}$ ) was added. The precipitate was washed by centrifugation, and then dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ to prepare the Co–MOF powder and CoFe–MOF powder.
Preparation of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ : the preparation of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ was similar to CoFe–MOF/NF, only TAA $(0.05\mathrm{~g},0.67\mathrm{~mol})\mathrm{}.$ ) was added.
Preparation of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ with different TAA contents: similar to the synthesis of $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{FeS}_{8}@\$ CoFe–MOF/NF, only the addition of TAA $_{(0.01\mathrm{~g~}}$ and $_{0.10\ \mathrm{g}}{\cdot}$ was changed. Mark as CoFe–MOF/NF ( $10\ \mathrm{\mg}$ TAA) and CoFe–MOF/NF ( $100\ \mathrm{\mg}$ TAA), respectively.
Synthesis of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ : similar to the synthesis of $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{FeS}_{8}@$ - CoFe–MOF/NF, only BDC was not added. Mark as $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
The morphology of the catalysts was analyzed by scanning electron microscope (SEM) (Hitachi, SU8010, Tokyo, Japan) at $15~\mathrm{kV}$ and transmission electron microscope (TEM) (Japan) with a $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV}}$ field emission gun. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was performed with $\mathrm{Cu-K}\mathfrak{a}$ radiation $(\lambda=1.54\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ). The energy-dispersive $\mathsf{X}$ -ray spectrometry (EDS) with an IXRF SDD 2610 EDS system. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed using a monochromatic Al- $\cdot\kappa\mathsf{\boldsymbol{a}}$ radiation source and a WSCAL-ab 220i-XL spectrometer. The peaks were recorded at a constant pass energy of $30{\mathrm{~eV}}.$ The residual pressure inside the analysis chamber was $6\times10^{-10}$ mbar. The C 1s main peak at $284.80~\mathrm{eV}$ was employed as the reference value to calibrate all the binding energy. Raman spectra were obtained on an InVia 1WU072 Raman spectrometer with $\lambda=532~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Inductively coupled plasma (ICP) was conducted on an avioTM 200 ICP-OES. Contact angle (CA) was performed with a Krüss contact angle analyser (DSA 100).
