## 2.5. In situ Raman spectroscopy test
This test was conducted in a custom-built Teflon cell. The electrode configuration was consistent with the electrochemical measurements except that the electrolyte was adjusted to 0.10 M KOH to reduce the impact of bubbles on spectral collection. The Raman spectra were collected at $532\mathrm{nm}$ laser power and a potential range of 0.90 to $2\:\mathsf{V}$ (vs. RHE).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalysts characterization
Previous reports and calculations showed that the preparation of p-p heterojunctions can effectively enhance the activities of OER and UOR [32]. Fig. S1 shows the M-S curves of CoFe–MOF/ NF, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ , which exhibit a negative slope, the typical characteristics of p-type semiconductors. The preparation process of CoFe–MOF/NF and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ is presented in Scheme 1. CoFe–MOF/NF was prepared by mixing metal precursors $\mathrm{~\mathsf~{~C~o~}~}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ) with BDC, and then dissolved in the DMF solution, followed by a simple solvothermal method. Different from CoFe–MOF/NF preparation, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ was obtained by adding an appropriate amount of TAA. During this process, the substrate NF can guide the catalyst to grow regularly on its surface, and ethanol as a capping agent can inhibit the agglomeration of the catalyst, thus ensuring that the as-prepared MOFs can obtain an ordered nanosheet structure. The optical photos show that the catalyst is uniformly distributed on the NF, and the color changes from brown (Co–MOF/NF) to light yellow (CoFe–MOF/NF) and finally to black $(\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F})$ (Fig. S2).
The morphologies of different catalysts were tested by SEM. In Fig. S3, the skeleton of the Blank NF presents a smooth surface. While Co–MOF/NF and CoFe–MOF/NF grow uniformly on NF and have similar nanosheet morphology, the addition of Fe does not destroy the morphology of pristine MOFs due to the similar atomic radii of Fe and Co (Fig. 1a and 1b). With the introduction of TAA, a small amount of aggregates adhered to the surface of ${\mathsf{C o F e-M O F}}/$ NF nanosheet (Fig. 1c). Interestingly, when the amount of TAA continued to increase, more small nanosheets adhered to the periphery of the CoFe–MOF/NF nanosheets (red mark), forming hierarchically regular Co8FeS8@CoFe–MOF/NF heterostructure arrays with tight interfaces (Fig. 1d and 1e) is helpful to facilitate charge transfer and increase the active area. The heterostructure array can be more clearly observed in Fig. S4a. After adding $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of TAA, the original nanosheet morphology completely disappeared, and only smaller-sized nanosheet morphologies were observed, which was caused by the complete formation of sulfide (XRD verification below) (Fig. 1f).
To check the details of the morphology, TEM and HRTEM were used to further verify the micromorphology. The boundaries of the different contrast regions show that the bright regions represent CoFe–MOF nanosheets (red mark), and the dark regions has smaller nanosheets (yellow mark) vertically distributed, which may be the generated $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ phase (Fig. $^{1\mathbf{g}}$ and S4b). HRTEM clearly observed that some small nanosheets were vertically distributed on the surface of large nanosheets (Fig. S4). As shown in Fig. 1h, HRTEM showed a distinct interface between the dark and bright regions, where the (400) and (311) planes of $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ match to $0.201\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{Re}=0.99\%)$ and $0.219~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ( $\mathrm{Re}=0.91\ \%$ lattice fringes, while the $0.208\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{Re}=0.96\%)$ and $0.238\mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{Re}=0.84\%)$ lattice fringes at the edges are attributed to $^{(7\ 1\ -1)}$ and $(80-1)$ planes of the CoFe–MOF, which further indicates the $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{Fe}S_{8}$ was attached to the CoFe–MOF/NF surface. Obviously, ultrathin $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ with an average size about $3{-}4\mathrm{nm}$ was attached to the surface of CoFe–MOF nanosheets to forming $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ heterostructure array (Fig. 1i).The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern of the $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ heterojunction can be well indexed to the planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{Fe}S_{8}$ and CoFe–MOF (Fig. 1j). Moreover, EDS mapping confirmed the uniform distribution of Co, Fe, O and C, while the S and N were discretely distributed, again indicating that $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ were dispersedly embedded on the surface of the CoFe–MOF nanosheets (Fig. 1k). Therefore, the analysis results of SEM and TEM confirm the formation of heterostructure arrays with a large number of dense phase interfaces in $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{\Omega}$ CoFe–MOF/NF, and the smaller nanosheets attached to the surface of CoFe–MOF/NF are $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ phase. The uneven distribution of charges at the phase interface leads to rapid charge transfer, which in turn tunes the electronic structure of active site to enhance the electrocatalytic activity [33-35]. In addition, the metal components in CoFe–MOF/NF and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ were quantitatively analyzed by ICP, and the results are shown in Table S1.
The formation mechnism and crytal structure of metal sulfides embedded on the surface of MOFs nanosheets were further systemayically analyzed by XRD. Control experiments showed that Co–MOF/NF and CoFe–MOF/NF exhibited characteristic peaks consistent with the simulated Co–MOF (CCDC: 153067) [30] in the absence of TAA (Fig. 2a, S5 and S6). However, pure $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaCo_{8}F e S_{8}}$ PDF # 29-0484) was prepared when TAA was present and BDC was absent (Figs. S9 and S10). Obviously, with the increase of TAA content, the new peaks of $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ at $15.44^{\circ}$ $\mathit{'}\mathrm{Re}\ =\ 0.13\ \%$ , $29.78^{\circ}$ $\mathrm{'Re}~=~0.067\%$ and $51.97^{\circ}$ $\mathrm{'Re}~=~0.038\%$ become more obvious, while the peak intensity of CoFe–MOF at $8.88^{\circ}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=0.23\%$ , $14.12^{\circ}$ ( $\mathrm{Re}=0.14\%$ , $15.82^{\circ}$ ( $\mathrm{Re}=0.13\%$ and $17.85^{\circ}$ ( $\mathrm{Re}=0.11\%$ ) decreases slightly, indicating that the amount of sulfide generated on the surface of MOFs can be regulated by the dosage of TAA (Fig. 2b, 2c and S7) (Note: the strong peak at $44.63^{\circ}$ $(\mathrm{Re}=0.045\%$ in all the XRD belongs to the characteristic peak of NF (JCPDS: 04–0850)). As shown in Fig. S8, further increasing the addition of TAA ( $[100~\mathrm{mg},$ can lead to the disappearance of the diffraction peaks of CoFe–MOF, indicating that CoFe–MOF is completely converted to $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ , corresponding to the SEM results. In the Raman spectra, several characteristic peaks $200\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}.$ – $800~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) can be observed in $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ compared with Co–MOF/NF and CoFe–MOF/NF (Fig. 2d), which can be attributed to the $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ phase, where $503~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\mathit{'}\mathrm{Re}=0.40\%$ weak peaks $(\mathsf{M O}_{\mathrm{x}})$ may be formed due to surface oxidation. The above results once again verify that $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ and CoFe–MOF were successfully combined on NF to form the $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{FeS}_{8}@C$ oFe–MOF/NF heterostructure arrays.
## 3.2. OER performance
The electrochemical performance of the catalysts was tested directly in alkaline solution. To demonstrate the excellent catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ the activities of blank NF, Co– MOF/NF, CoFe–MOF/NF, and $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}/\mathsf{N F}$ were also compared. The LSV curves of the five catalysts are shown in Fig. 4a. As expected, the OER performance of blank NF is negligible, while the OER efficiency of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ is the highest. Notably, ${\mathsf{C o}}{-}{\mathsf{M O F}}/$ NF, CoFe–MOF/NF, and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ exhibit weaker performance compared to $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ , suggesting that sulfides play an important role in promoting OER electrocatalytic activity of CoFe–MOF. This improvement can be attributed to synergistic effects between different metals (Co and Fe) and different substances $\mathrm{\Co_{8}F e S_{8}}$ and CoFe–MOF), as well as heterojunction arrays with tight phase interfaces and electron-rich structures. Furthermore, the OER catalytic activity of CoFe–MOF/NF was measured with different TAA (Fig. S12 and Table S2). According to the test results, the optimal OER activity and kinetic process were obtained when $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of TAA was added. More importantly, $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{C o F e-}$ MOF/NF exhibits a low overpotential of $213~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 4b), which is superior to the OER performance of most reported transition metal-catalytic materials (Table S5), confirming that the functionalization of MOFs can serve as efficient OERactivity electrocatalysts. The current densities at $1.50~\mathrm{V}$ and the overpotentials at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for these catalysts are summarized in Fig. 4c, where $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O I}$ F/NF has a high current density of $196.48\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{Re}=1.02\%)$ and a low overpotential of $252\mathrm{mV}$ ( $\mathrm{Re}=0.79\%$ , which is significantly better than that of Co–MOF/NF $(21.54~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=9.28\%$ ) and $316~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=0.95\%)$ ), CoFe– MOF/NF (62.05 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{Re}}&{{}=}&{3.22\%)}\end{array}$ and $283~\mathrm{\mV}$ $(\mathrm{Re}=0.71\%)$ ), and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ $24.93\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=8.02\%\$ and $319~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathrm{\cdotRe}~=~0.94\%)$ ), directly confirming that the coupling of $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}$ and CoFe–MOF can significantly enhance the OER activity. In order to exclude the effect of Ni present in NF on the catalytic performance, a control experiment was carried out for verification. When the catalyst was supported the carbon paper (CP) electrode $(1\ \mathrm{mg\cm^{-2}},\$ for OER performance test, it is clearly found that the performance of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/C P}$ is still better than that of CoFe–MOF/CP and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/C P}$ , which verifies that the heterojunction array can indeed improve the catalytic performance (Figs. S13a and S13b). However, the performance and kinetics of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ are still better than $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/}$ CP, which also indicates that the presence of NF can indeed improve the activity of the catalyst (Figs. S13c and S13d).
Furthermore, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ also exhibits excellent OER durability. As shown in Fig. 4f, the performance of $\mathrm{Co}_{8}\mathrm{FeS}_{8}@-$ CoFe–MOF/NF remains unchanged for $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ . Meanwhile, the multistep chronopotentiometric curve from $20\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ to $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ also verifies the high corrosion resistance and rapid gas release capability, indicating excellent durability. Combining the above advantages, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/}$ NF can act as efficient and robust OER electrocatalysts in alkaline solution.
The structural transitions of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ and CoFe– MOF/NF during OER were analyzed using in situ Raman spectroscopy in the potential range from OCP up to $2\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE) Fig. S15. Fig. 5a and 5b show that two distinct Raman peaks appear at approximately 547 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{Re}\ \mathrm{~\ensuremath~{~=~}~}0.55\%)$ and $464~\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{Re}~=~0.43\%$ with increasing potential, which are attributed to the bending vibrations of M-O $\operatorname{E}_{\mathrm{g}},$ $\gamma$ -MOOH) and stretching vibrations of MOOH $\cdot_{\mathbf{A}_{1\mathbf{g}}},$ $\upbeta$ -MOOH) [28]. It is particularly remarkable that $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N!}$ F exhibits a weak peak signal $(503~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) after immersion in KOH solution without applied potential or at low potential, which is caused by the formation of the active intermediate $\mathsf{M O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ (Co is slightly oxidized to $\mathsf{C o O}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{.}$ , suggesting that the active phase was easy to form [49,50]. Then, when the potential exceeds $1.30{\mathrm{V}}$ (Fig. S16a), $\mathsf{M O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ can be directly converted into $\upbeta$ -MOOH, simultaneously $\gamma$ -MOOH is also gradually generated. The $\upbeta$ -MOOH is generally considered to be a more active phase than $\gamma$ -MOOH [51,52]. When more $\upbeta$ -MOOH is produced, the B/A $_{\cdot}{\mathrm{\beta-MOOH/}}\gamma$ -MOOH) values at different potentials should be the highest. Fig. S16f shows that the $\mathsf{B}/\mathsf{A}$ value in $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{C o F e-}$ MOF/NF is higher than that in CoFe–MOF/NF at voltages from $1.30{\mathrm{V}}$ to $1.70{\mathrm{V}}$ (vs. RHE), indicating that the introduction of sulfide is more easy to generate more $\upbeta$ -MOOH active phase.
To understand the intrinsic catalytic activity of $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{C o F e}-$ MOF/NF, the catalysts were evaluated by ECSA and TOF. ECSA is determined by the $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ associated with different scan rates (Fig. S17) [53,54]. As shown in Fig. 5c and 5d, $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{C o F e-}$ MOF/NF $3.05~\mathrm{mF}\quad\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{Re}}&{{}=}&{0.33\%)}\end{array}$ and $76.25\quad{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2}$ $(\mathrm{Re}~=~0.33\%)$ ) has higher $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ and ECSA values than blank NF $(1.51~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=0.66\%$ and $37.75~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=0.66\%)$ ), Co– MOF/NF ( $1.72\mathrm{mF}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathrm{Re}}&{{}=}&{0.58\ \%)}\end{array}$ and $43.00~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $(\mathrm{Re}~=~0.58~\%)$ ), CoFe–MOF/NF $\cdot1.78~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $(\mathrm{Re}~=~0.56\%)$ and $44.50~\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ $\mathrm{(Re~\ensuremath~{~=~}~}0.56\%)$ ), and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ $(2.40~\mathrm{mF}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $(\mathrm{Re}~=~0.42\%)$ and $60.00~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ $(\mathrm{Re}~=~0.42\%)$ ), respectively. The enlargement of the ECSA benefits from the nanosheet heterojunction array of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ , allowing to provide more active sites. Moreover, compared with blank NF, Co–MOF/NF, CoFe–MOF/NF, and ${\sf C o}_{8}\mathrm{FeS}_{8}/{\sf N F},$ $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ still has the highest TOF value, indicating that the intrinsic catalytic performance is very high (Fig. 5d and S18-S22). In addition to ECSA and TOF, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ also maintains the best performance after ECSA normalized LSV curves, which again implies the highest intrinsic OER activity among the five catalysts (Fig. 5e). These results above strongly verify that the combination of sulfide and MOFs can effectively promote intrinsic OER activity. Furthermore, the FE of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ reaches $93.58\%$ ( $\mathrm{Re}=3.52\%)$ , further highlighting the high OER catalytic activity (Electrochemical measurements). In addition to optimizing these features, the surface properties of heterojunctions can also vary widely. The surface wettability of these catalysts was further investigated by CA measurements. According to previous reports, sulfide has good hydrophilicity [55]. Fortunately, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ heterojunction has the lowest CA value (Fig. S23), which can improve the contact with the electrolyte as well as promote gas evolution, thus leading to the improvement of catalytic activity [22,56]. As a result, the $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ remained stable at different current densities, while the generated gas rapidly leaves the surface (Fig. 4f), verifying the feasibility of practical applications.
## 3.3. HER performance and water splitting
The HER activity of the prepared catalysts was also further evaluated in alkaline solution. The overpotential of $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{C o F e-}$ MOF/NF is $191~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathit{T e}=1.05\%$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , which is lower than of CoFe–MOF/NF $\cdot248\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\mV}}$ $\mathrm{'Re}~=~0.81\%$ ) and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ $223~\mathrm{mV}$ $\mathrm{'Re}=0.89\%)$ ) (Fig. 6a). Besides, the low Tafel value verifies the fast HER reaction kinetics (Fig. 6b). And the presence of a small semicircle diameter in the EIS indicates the smallest charge transfer resistance (Fig. 6c). More importantly, its activity remained stable within $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ , showing good durability (Fig. 6d). Encouraged by the excellent HER and OER performance of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F},$ , its water splitting performance was tested directly as cathode and anode. As shown in Fig. 6e, only a low cell voltage of $1.62\mathrm{~V~}$ (without iR compensation) is required to drive water decomposition at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ . Compared with other catalysts (Table S7), such as, $\mathsf{N i F e{-}L D H@N i_{2}S_{3}/N F}$ (1.74 V) [58], MnCo-G/NF (1.70 V) [59], NiFe–LDH $\boldsymbol{I}\mathrm{FeCoS}_{2}/\mathrm{CC}$ (1.74 V) [60], CoMnCH/NF (1.68 V) [61], CoIIFe-ONC (1.71 V) [62], the ${\sf C o}_{8}\mathrm{Fe}{\cal S}_{8}@\cdots$ - CoFe–MOF/NF is also in an upstream position. And chronopotentiometry curve shows that the catalyst system maintains long term stable dissociation at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 6f). These all indicate that $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ heterostructure arrays has great potential for application in water splitting systems.
## 3.4. UOR performance and urea splitting
Although the $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ heterostructure exhibits excellent OER and HER catalytic performance under alkaline conditions, the hydrogen production efficiency fails to meet practical industrial demands. Therefore, we hope to use UOR instead of OER to improve hydrogen production efficiency. The UOR activity of these catalysts was tested in $1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH with $0.33\mathrm{~M~}$ urea. As shown in Fig. S27, compared with $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ $(1.30~\mathrm{mV})$ and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ $\cdot1.12\mathrm{\mV},$ , although the onset potential of Co–MOF/NF $1.11\mathrm{\mV},$ and CoFe–MOF/NF $1.11\mathrm{\mV},$ is low, the performance gradually decreases with the increase of potential (competing fiercely with OER). However, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ still have good activity, which indicates that the introduction of sulfide can greatly improve the UOR activity and UOR is more favorable than OER at low potentials (Fig. 7a). And in order to achieve real wastewater treatment, some inorganic and organic substances are added to the electrolyte to simulate the actual wastewater situation, Fig. S28 shows that the prepared $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ heterojunction catalyst still has excellent catalytic performance. Additionally, using the Tafel slope to study the UOR kinetics of different electrocatalysts. The Tafel value of $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ $17.72\ m\lor\mathrm{~dec}^{-1}$ ) is very low, which is much smaller than that of Co–MOF/NF $(228.71\ \mathrm{\mV}\ \mathrm{\dec}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{Re}~=~0.87\%)$ ), CoFe–MOF/NF ( $119.25\mathrm{~mV}$ dec1 $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{(Re}}&{{}}&{1.68\ \%)_{.}^{\cdot}}\end{array}$ ) and $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}/N F}$ $\cdot54.45\mathrm{\mV\dec}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{Re}\:=\:1.84\:\%)$ ) (Fig. 7b, 7c and S27). And compared with the reported catalysts, the performance and kinetics have absolute advantages (Table S6), full demonstrating that the constructed heterojunction catalysts have great potential for practical UOR applications. Fig. 7c compares the difference in UOR and OER overpotentials at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , and the UOR overpotential for $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ is significantly reduced and at the lowest level. This result suggests that replacing OER with UOR is a good option for produce hydrogen with low energy consumption. Notably, the catalyst exhibits excellent decomposition activity in urea electrolysis, requiring only a potential of $1.55~\mathrm{~V~}$ (without iR compensation) to drive urea decomposition at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is $70\mathrm{mV}$ lower than the water splitting potential (Fig. 7d). In addition, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ also has good urea splitting performance (Table S7). And, as shown in Fig. 7e and 7f, $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ exhibits long term stability for both UOR and overall urea splitting. Therefore, the electrochemical studies of these systems collectively validate that $\mathsf{C o}_{8}\mathsf{F e S}_{8}@\mathsf{C o F e}-$ MOF/NF is a very promising electrocatalyst for hydrogen production.
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, we have achieved the goal of attaching sulfide to the surface of CoFe–MOF/NF nanosheets to prepare ${\sf C o}_{8}\mathrm{Fe}{\cal S}_{8}@\cdots$ - CoFe–MOF/NF heterostructure arrays with rich phase interfaces. Compared with MOFs based catalysts, the introduction of sulfide can not only adjust the morphology of the catalyst and the electronic environment of metal ions, but also improve the surface properties of the catalyst. According to the characterization results, the electron rich heterojunction catalyst can enhance the adsorption of oxygenated intermediates to optimize the rate-limiting step and easily generate active phase. As OER and UOR electrocatalysts, overpotential of $213\mathrm{~m~V~}$ and potential of $1.311\mathrm{~V~}$ are required at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively. And for the bifunctional electrocatalyst for overall water and urea splitting, driving a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ requires cell potentials of $1.62\mathrm{V}$ and $1.55\mathrm{V}$ (without iR compensated), respectively. This also proves that the prepared $\mathrm{Co_{8}F e S_{8}@C o F e-M O F/N F}$ is an excellent bifunctional catalyst, and UOR instead of OER is a low-energy hydrogen production method. This work provides a feasible approach to design bifunctional electrocatalyst heterojunction materials with excellent hydrogen production performance, which is expected to provide a reference for the design and research of multifunctional electrocatalyst in the future.
